/**
  @page examples-io-xdmf Exporting a time-dependent field
  @brief Write an analytic vector field and inspect its snapshots in ParaView.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-io-xdmf-intro Field and time sampling

  On the unit square, consider the prescribed vector field
  @f[
    \mathbf{u}(x,y,t)=
    \begin{pmatrix}
      \sin(2\pi(x+t))\\
      \cos(2\pi(y-t))
    \end{pmatrix}.
  @f]
  Its components vary periodically in space and time. This known field provides
  a direct check of time-series output: each exported nodal value can be
  compared with the formula at the recorded time.

  @section examples-io-xdmf-setup Define the mesh and space

  A grid with 32 vertices per direction gives 1,024 vertices and 1,922 triangles.
  The vector-valued @f$P_1@f$ space has two components. Forty intervals of
  length @f$\Delta t=0.05@f$ produce 41 snapshots, including @f$t=0@f$ and
  @f$t=2@f$.

  @snippet IO/XDMF.cpp xdmf-setup

  @section examples-io-xdmf-writer Register the field

  The writer observes the mesh and grid function. Both objects remain alive
  throughout the output loop. The explicit name **u** is the field name shown
  in ParaView.

  @snippet IO/XDMF.cpp xdmf-writer

  @section examples-io-xdmf-timeloop Interpolate and write each snapshot

  Each assignment interpolates the analytic field at the current time.
  `write(t).flush()` records that state and updates the output files before
  the next iteration.

  @snippet IO/XDMF.cpp xdmf-loop

  @section examples-io-xdmf-output Keep the output files together

  `TimeEvolution.xdmf` describes the temporal collection and references the
  HDF5 mesh and field files. Copy the metadata and its referenced HDF5 files
  together when moving the result. The mesh is stored once under the default
  static-mesh policy; each time step adds field data.

  @section examples-io-xdmf-paraview Inspect the time series

  1. Open `TimeEvolution.xdmf` in ParaView and select **Apply**.
  2. Color by **u**, selecting **Magnitude** for the vector norm.
  3. Apply **Glyph**, select **u** as the orientation array and choose a sparse
     sampling so the arrows remain legible.
  4. Use the time controls to inspect the 41 states between zero and two.

  @image html XDMF.png "Vector magnitude and direction at two exported times."

  The figures use a common magnitude scale from zero to @f$\sqrt{2}@f$.
  The two times reveal the change in vector direction as well as magnitude.

  @section examples-io-xdmf-full Build and run

  Build the `RodinIOXDMF` target and run the executable in a scratch directory.
  The complete program is:

  @snippet IO/XDMF.cpp xdmf-source

  @example IO/XDMF.cpp
  @m_examplenavigation{examples-io-xdmf,IO/}
  @m_footernavigation
*/
